{"role": "assistant", "content": "Certainly! Here's an example of how to create an HTTP response with a custom status code, headers, and body using the `warp::reply::response()` function:

```
use warp::{Filter, reply::Response};

// Define a handler function to handle the request
async fn custom_response() -> Response {
    // Set the status code to 418 (I'm a teapot)
    let status_code = warp::http::StatusCode::IM_A_TEAPOT;

    // Create a HashMap of custom headers and values to be included in the response
    let mut headers = warp::http::HeaderMap::new();
    headers.insert("X-Custom-Header", "Hello, World!".parse().unwrap());

    // Define the response body as a string
    let response_body = "This is a custom response with a custom status code, headers, and body!";

    // Create the response object using the `warp::reply::response()` function
    Response::builder()
        .status(status_code)
        .headers(headers)
        .body(response_body.into())
        .unwrap()
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Define the route using the `path` filter
    let custom_route = warp::path("custom")
        .map(|| custom_response());

    // Start the server and bind to port 8000
    warp::serve(custom_route)
        .run(([127, 0, 0, 1], 8000))
        .await;
}
```

In this example, we define the `custom_response()` handler function, which returns a `warp::reply::Response` object. 

Within the function, we set the status code to 418 using the `warp::http::StatusCode` enum, which represents HTTP status codes. We also define a set of custom headers using a `warp::http::HeaderMap` object, adding an example header named "X-Custom-Header" with a value of "Hello, World!".

The response body is defined as a string literal. Note that in order to convert this string to a type that can be used as a response body, we must call the `into()` method on it.

Finally, we construct the response object using the `warp::reply::response()` function, passing in the status code, headers, and body as arguments to the `Response::builder()` method. We then return this response object from the `custom_response()` function.

When a client makes a GET request to "http://localhost:8000/custom", the `custom_response()` function will be called and a custom HTTP response with a status code of 418, a custom header, and a custom response body will be sent back to the client."},
