{"role": "assistant", "content": "It's possible that the `total` value is not being updated correctly because of race conditions that can occur if multiple edits happen too quickly. For example, if two users try to edit the same document at the same time, both of their updates may not be applied correctly due to the way Firestore handles concurrency.

To prevent this issue, you can use a Firestore transaction to ensure that the document is updated atomically without overwriting any changes made by other clients.

Here's an example of how you could modify your `initUserExpenses` function to use a transaction:

```kotlin
fun initUserExpenses(
    name: String,
    quantity: Int,
    expense: Double,
    collectionName: String,
    documentId: String
) {
    val db = FirebaseFirestore.getInstance()
    val docRef = db.collection("expenses")
        .document("Kantin")
        .collection(collectionName)
        .document(documentId)

    val initData = mapOf(
        "name" to name,
        "quantity" to quantity,
        "expenses" to expense
    )

    db.runTransaction { transaction ->
        val document = transaction.get(docRef)

        if (!document.exists()) {
            // Create the document if it doesn't exist
            transaction.set(docRef, initData)
        } else {
            // Update the existing document
            val names = document.getList<String>("name") ?: emptyList()
            val quantities = document.getList<Int>("quantity") ?: emptyList()
            val expenses = document.getList<Double>("expenses") ?: emptyList()

            val itemIndex = names.indexOf(name)

            if (itemIndex != -1) {
                // Update existing item
                val updatedQuantities = quantities.toMutableList()
                updatedQuantities[itemIndex] += quantity

                val updatedExpenses = expenses.toMutableList()
                updatedExpenses[itemIndex] += expense

                // Compute the total expenses
                val totalExpenses = updatedExpenses.sum()

                // Update the document
                transaction.update(docRef, mapOf(
                    "quantity" to updatedQuantities,
                    "expenses" to updatedExpenses,
                    "total" to totalExpenses
                ))
            } else {
                // Add new item
                val updatedNames = names.toMutableList()
                updatedNames.add(name)

                val updatedQuantities = quantities.toMutableList()
                updatedQuantities.add(quantity)

                val updatedExpenses = expenses.toMutableList()
                updatedExpenses.add(expense)

                // Compute the total expenses
                val totalExpenses = updatedExpenses.sum()

                // Update the document
                transaction.update(docRef, mapOf(
                    "name" to updatedNames,
                    "quantity" to updatedQuantities,
                    "expenses" to updatedExpenses,
                    "total" to totalExpenses
                ))
            }
        }

        null
    }
    .addOnSuccessListener {
        Log.d("TAG", "Document updated successfully")
    }
    .addOnFailureListener { exception ->
        Log.e("TAG", "Error updating document", exception)
    }
}
```

This code uses a Firestore transaction to make sure that the document is updated atomically. First, it checks if the document exists, and if it does, it retrieves the existing values of the "name", "quantity", and "expenses" fields. It then checks if the item with the given name already exists in the document, and if it does, it updates the quantities and expenses accordingly. If the item doesn't exist, it adds a new item.

After updating the document, the function logs a success message if the transaction completed successfully, or an error message if there was an error."},
