{"role": "assistant", "content": "@EnableEurekaClient is specific to Netflix Eureka and is used to register the microservice to the Eureka Server. On the other hand, @EnableDiscoveryClient is a common annotation that can be used to enable service discovery for any service registry, including Eureka, Consul, and Zookeeper. However, if you are using Eureka as a service registry, it is recommended to use @EnableEurekaClient instead of @EnableDiscoveryClient to take full advantage of the Eureka-specific features."},
{"role": "user", "content": "how to register keycloak authoriation server in spring"},
{"role": "assistant", "content": "To register Keycloak authorization server in Spring, you need to follow the below steps:

1. Firstly, add the Keycloak Spring Boot starter dependency in your project's build.gradle or pom.xml file. This can be done by adding the following code in the dependency tag:

For Gradle:

```
dependencies {
    compile group: 'org.keycloak', name: 'keycloak-spring-boot-starter', version: '15.0.2'
}
```

For Maven:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
    <artifactId>keycloak-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>15.0.2</version>
</dependency>
```

2. Next, you need to configure Keycloak in your Spring Boot application by specifying the Keycloak server details in the application.properties file. Here's an example:

```
keycloak.auth-server-url=http://localhost:8080/auth
keycloak.realm=myrealm
keycloak.resource=myclient
keycloak.credentials.secret=mysecret
keycloak.ssl-required=external
keycloak.use-resource-role-mappings=true
```

3. Now, you should enable the Keycloak adapter in your Spring Boot application by annotating your main application class with the @EnableWebSecurity and @KeycloakConfiguration annotations. Here's an example:

```
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableWebSecurity
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class MyAppApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyAppApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

4. Finally, you need to configure the security settings specific to your application by extending the KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter class and overriding its methods as per your requirements. Here's an example:

```
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class SecurityConfig extends KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Autowired
    public void configureGlobal(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) {
        KeycloakAuthenticationProvider keycloakAuthenticationProvider = keycloakAuthenticationProvider();
        auth.authenticationProvider(keycloakAuthenticationProvider);
    }

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        super.configure(http);
        http.authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/myprotected/**").hasRole("user")
                .anyRequest().permitAll();
    }
}
```

These steps should help you register Keycloak authorization server in your Spring Boot application."},
