    <artifactId>keycloak-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>15.0.2</version>
</dependency>
```

2. Configure Keycloak properties: Configure Keycloak properties in the application.properties or application.yml file. Here is an example:

```
# Keycloak properties
keycloak.auth-server-url=http://localhost:8080/auth
keycloak.realm=myrealm
keycloak.resource=myclient
keycloak.credentials.secret=mysecret
keycloak.ssl-required=external
keycloak.use-resource-role-mappings=true
keycloak.security-constraints[0].authRoles[0]=user
keycloak.security-constraints[0].securityCollections[0].patterns[0]=/myprotected/*
```

3. Enable and configure Keycloak within your application: Enable Keycloak in your main Spring Boot class by adding the following annotations: @EnableWebSecurity, @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true) and @KeycloakConfiguration

```
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class KeycloakApplication extends KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    /**
     * Registers the KeycloakAuthenticationProvider with the authentication manager.
     */
    @Autowired
    public void configureGlobal(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) throws Exception {
        KeycloakAuthenticationProvider keycloakAuthProvider = keycloakAuthenticationProvider();
        auth.authenticationProvider(keycloakAuthProvider);
    }

    /**
     * Defines the session authentication strategy.
     */
    @Bean
    @Override
    protected SessionAuthenticationStrategy sessionAuthenticationStrategy() {
        return new RegisterSessionAuthenticationStrategy(new SessionRegistryImpl());
    }

    /**
     * Configures the HttpSecurity, specifying URL patterns and roles that are allowed
     * to access these URL patterns.
     */
    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        super.configure(http);
        http
                .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/myprotected/**").hasRole("user")
                .anyRequest().permitAll();
    }
}
```

4. Use Keycloak for Application Security: You can use Keycloak for application security by using the KeycloakAuthenticationToken in your application's controllers or services.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getAllUsers(Authentication authentication) {
        KeycloakAuthenticationToken authenticationToken = (KeycloakAuthenticationToken) authentication;
        AccessToken accessToken = authenticationToken.getAccount().getKeycloakSecurityContext().getToken();
        // use accessToken to perform authorization or extract user information
    }

    @PostMapping
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('admin')")
    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        // create user
    }
}
```

These are the basic steps you need to follow to use Keycloak provider in Spring Boot. Keycloak acts as the identity provider and authenticates the users for your application, which results in a more secure application."}
