{"role": "assistant", "content": "B.1. 

To create an event object that detects whether the alt, ctrl or shift key has been pressed, you can use the onkeydown event in JavaScript. Here's an example code snippet:

document.onkeydown = function(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  var keyPressed = String.fromCharCode(event.keyCode).toLowerCase();
  var altKeyPressed = event.altKey;
  var ctrlKeyPressed = event.ctrlKey;
  var shiftKeyPressed = event.shiftKey;

  console.log("Key pressed: " + keyPressed);
  console.log("Alt key pressed: " + altKeyPressed);
  console.log("Ctrl key pressed: " + ctrlKeyPressed);
  console.log("Shift key pressed: " + shiftKeyPressed);
};

This code listens for the onkeydown event on the entire document, and when a key is pressed, it retrieves the ASCII code of the key and determines whether the alt, ctrl or shift key was pressed using the event object properties. It then logs this information to the console.

B.2.

To prevent the context menu from being displayed when the user right-clicks the mouse, you can use the oncontextmenu event in JavaScript. Here's an example code snippet:

document.oncontextmenu = function(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
};

This code disables the default context menu behavior by calling the preventDefault() method on the event object.

B.3.

To create a simple form that displays a confirmation message and stops or continues form submission based on user action, as well as a custom "timeout" event that fires after 30 seconds of inactivity, you can use the following code:

HTML:
<form id="myForm">
  <label for="nameInput">Name:</label>
  <input type="text" id="nameInput" name="nameInput">
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>

JavaScript:
var form = document.getElementById("myForm");

form.addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
  var confirmSubmission = confirm("Are you sure you want to submit the form?");
  if (!confirmSubmission) {
    event.preventDefault();
  }
});

var timeoutId;

form.addEventListener("input", function(event) {
  clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  timeoutId = setTimeout(function() {
    form.dispatchEvent(new Event("timeout"));
  }, 30000);
});

form.addEventListener("timeout", function(event) {
  alert("You haven't entered any data in the last 30 seconds.");
});

In this code, the form element is retrieved using the getElementById() method, and event listeners are added to it. When the form is submitted, a confirmation message is displayed using the confirm() method, and if the user clicks "Cancel", the event's preventDefault() method is called to stop the form submission.

To create the custom "timeout" event, an event listener is added to the form's input event, which is fired whenever the user types something in the input field. A timeout ID is stored in a variable, and every time the input event is fired, the clearTimeout() method is called to clear any previously set timeouts, and a new timeout is set using the setTimeout() method. This timeout calls the form's dispatchEvent() method with a new Event object that has a type of "timeout".

Finally, when the "timeout" event is fired, an alert message is displayed to the user."}
